WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.640
Congratulations, SCP Explained fans - you've
just scored yourselves front row seats to

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.520
the freak show.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.600
Hey, don't be so nervous - I know clowns don't
necessarily have the best rep these days - thank

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.680
you very much, John Wayne Gacy and Stephen
King - but I guarantee you I'm not like the

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.400
other clowns at Herman Fuller's Circus of
the Disquieting, I'm as human as you are.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.560
Well, okay, that's potentially debatable,
depending on how stingy you are with your

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.920
definitions of what exactly counts, but we'll
get into that later.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.640
See, I'm what they call an anomalous humanoid,
known to the eggheads who work for Miss Essie

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.760
P. Foundation as SCP-2094, object class Euclid,
whatever the heck that means.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.880
My friends call me Barney, on account of the
fact that's my government name, but if you're

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.720
a fan of the Circus you probably know me better
by my stage name: Motormouth.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.000
Pretty fitting nickname, ain’t it?

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.040
I tried to make “Barney the Bottomless”
happen in the early days of my career, but

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:48.480
it wasn't catchy enough to stick.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.920
Then one day my buddy Quincy threatened to
barf a swarm of bees on my face if I didn't

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.960
shut my motormouth, and that's been my stage
name ever since.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.400
Now that we're properly introduced, you're
probably wondering what cool tricks I have

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.000
up my sleeve to get me that nifty Essie P.
designation.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.760
Well, it's not because of my heightened manual
dexterity, my juggling skills, my accordion

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.520
playing prowess, or even my nearly perfect
impression of Jane Fonda as Barbarella - so

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.760
if you guessed any of those, you're way off
base.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.200
See, I'm the subject of a quote-unquote “spatial
anomaly”, accessible through my mouth and

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:17.760
throat.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.160
In plain English, I've got a second stomach
- anything I swallow disappears into a pocket

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.960
dimension made of anomalously elastic intestinal
tissue, where it's stored until I need it

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:27.440
again.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.200
If there's a limit to how much I can store
in there, I haven't found it yet.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.360
The amount of stuff in there has no effect
on my overall weight or my ability to move,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.240
once it's in there it's just kinda gone until
I want it back.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.240
On top of that, my lower jaw can be stretched
up to 6 feet – that's 2 meters for those

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.600
of you weirdos who use the metric system - in
any direction.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.840
So in addition to being a juggler and a clown,
I also made a killing as a geek at Herman

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:50.800
Fuller's Circus.

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.360
I don't mean geek as in “owns a signed James
T Kirk funko pop that's still in mint condition”,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.280
I mean in the sense of somebody who gets paid
to eat weird junk as a form of performance,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.920
although I have been accused by some of being
both.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.040
Now you're probably thinking, “Motormouth,
my pal, my good buddy, there's so many careers

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.520
you could've gone into with that very specific
set of skills.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.520
Garbage disposal, one-man moving van service,
left luggage attendant at grand central station,

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.800
sky's the limit, man!”

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.800
Well, sure, but I love the circus!

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.640
They're the only family I've got in this world.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.680
I came to them when I was just a wide-eyed
punk kid of eight years old, and they took

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.760
me in like I was their own flesh and blood.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:25.280
Yes, believe it or not, I wasn't actually
born into the circus life.

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.520
A lot of circus and performing arts folk are
born into the industry - 'born in a costume

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.640
trunk' as they say - but unfortunately, that's
not me.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.080
I was born in New York City on April 10th,
1976.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.000
My dad had took off long before I showed up,
leaving me in the care of my dear old mom.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.920
My special abilities manifested early and
my mom recognized them right away.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.560
You know how little kids are always putting
things in their mouths, right?

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:51.360
It's cute when it's a block or an action figure,
but less so when it's the entire coffee table.

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.600
Yeah, my mom was fully aware of what I could
do, and she hated my guts for it.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.120
Not only had her baby daddy flown the coop,
but the kid she was raising was a freak of

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.280
nature who she could barely stand to look
at.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.400
And trust me, the feelings of resentment were
totally mutual.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.320
One of my favorite pastimes as a youngster
was to go out and find me as many small animals

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.000
as I could get my little hands on, then go
back inside while mom was laid out on the

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.120
parlor chair sleeping off one of her special
grown-up headaches she got from downing a

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.360
bottle and a half of her special grown-up
juice.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:21.200
I'd make like I was gonna innocently ask her
a question, smiling one of those adorable

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.720
little 8-year-old smiles, then instead of
saying anything I'd just barf up a couple

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.400
dozen rats onto her lap.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.160
Despite how much I hated living at home, it
never occurred to me that I could leave.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:31.680
I was a pretty wide-eyed kid.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.680
Mom kept me isolated from the wider world,
I guess outta fear that I'd eat somebody.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.040
But one night, as I was going to bed, I met
Manny.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:39.680
Who's Manny?

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:44.560
Come on, if you know anything about the Circus
of the Disquieting, you know Manny.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.400
Don’t you guys already have a video on that?

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.440
Manny’s short for “the Man With the Upside
Down Face.”

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.200
He was Herman Fuller's right hand, in charge
of a lot of the backstage stuff, including,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.080
shall we say, “talent acquisition.”

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.840
Who knows how he figured out where I was or
that I could do what I could do, but just

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.160
like magic, he showed up at my window.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.760
Should I have been worried that a man with
an upside-down face was at my window?

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:06.320
Probably.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.840
But I guess I was just happy to see someone
who was more of a freak than me!

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.600
Manny offered me a place in a real family,
where I wouldn't be cooped up.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.920
He said freaks belonged out in the world,
sharing their talents, making people laugh

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.920
and cry and puke all over the place.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.400
I didn't even hesitate, I took him up on his
offer.

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.000
Mommy dearest was so deep in the drink that
she didn't notice I left.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.040
And that was that!

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.320
From that day forward I was a member of Herman
Fuller's family.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.960
I never looked back, not even once.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.120
Now, it may also surprise you to know that
I wasn't always the picture of confidence

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.000
I am today.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.880
My first job with the circus was as the human
clown car.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.560
As a kid I didn't have a lot of stage presence,
and I didn't talk much, so the natural thing

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.720
to do was to send me out to the ring and let
the clown troupe come marching outta my mouth,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.800
to the surprise and delight of the audience.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:52.800
I learned juggling from a guy named Scythe.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.040
He was a human pin cushion, but like most
of us at Herman's, his abilities were way

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.080
beyond what you'd see at any common or garden
circus.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.080
Scythe was incredible at what he did, you
could stick a shiv in every square inch of

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.680
this guy and he'd never bat an eye!

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:09.120
Total jerk, though, and awful with kids, so
how he got the job of training me in the ways

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.200
of the juggler is totally beyond me.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.200
If I so much as talked out of turn, he'd bean
me in the head with a juggling pin.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:17.360
Not hard, just enough to hurt.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.240
I know that sounds bad, but at least it toughened
me up.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.120
I think Scythe was just jealous of me in hindsight
- I was a natural and I think he saw me as

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.880
a threat.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.680
Scythe eventually died in a fire stunt gone
wrong.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.480
They held a funeral for him, but I was busy
that day.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.360
Clowning I learned from a guy called Scruffy
McGrubbins.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:36.560
Good ol' Scruffy.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.800
He had all the grace and countenance of a
chain-smoking grizzly bear, and he was worth

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.600
more to me than all the grandmothers I never
had.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.040
He never talked to me like I was stupid, the
way most people talk to kids.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.360
Scruffy took me seriously, and he imparted
unto me all the knowledge he'd picked up in

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.880
his years in the circus trade.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.120
He was a very wise man as clowns go.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.200
He never minced words, never messed around
with small talk, he just jumped straight into

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.280
a conversation and expected you to catch up.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.960
I never knew what happened to that guy.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.400
I've heard he quit the clown biz and settled
down in a nice apartment in Kent.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.880
I hope he's having a good day, wherever he
is.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.520
So I did the juggling thing for a while - tossing
around balls and pins and other junk and catching

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.240
it in my mouth.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.440
Sometimes I'd put a plant in the audience,
call them up and swallow them whole as part

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:18.160
of the act.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:21.760
Eventually, I started working on a double
act with another freak by the name of Theo.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:25.920
See, Theo could flip himself inside-out, but
he was also a pretty skilled acrobat, so he'd

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.600
do a high dive jump over the audience, pull
his insides outside, then aim himself down

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:30.240
my gullet.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.560
It was a pretty great act if I do say so myself.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.520
Theo and I even dated for a while - what can
I say, I find guys just as fun to swallow

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:38.000
as gals.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.920
But after he and I broke it off, I decided
I needed to change up my act.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.640
That's when it hit me: Motormouth.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.200
Picture this - here's me, an assistant on
either side ready to pull my mouth open.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.760
Then a Chrysler comes barreling into the big
top, hits a ramp, and drives right through

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:55.680
my mouth into my second stomach.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.520
It was a dangerous act - I'm magic, sure,
but not immune to being run over - but it

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:01.840
was the best act I ever came up with.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.120
Oh, I could tell you some stories about my
time with the circus.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.440
There was the time my tapeworm started talking
to me, the time I was involved in overthrowing

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.200
Herman Fuller himself and installing Icky
as our new ringmaster, the time I hooked up

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.280
with one of the non-human&nbsp;
Capital-C Clowns...though&nbsp;

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.400
this is a family-friendly channel, so maybe

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:19.680
not that one.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.280
That's not what you wanna hear, though, is
it?

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.840
You'd rather hear about how I got myself captured
and locked up by Miss Essie P. Foundation.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:29.840
Alright, why not.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.000
I've already spilled the rest of my backstory.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.160
So, Manny - you remember Manny, from earlier,
right?

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.480
- He saved a lot of kids like me, and when
he did he usually put me in charge of teaching

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:39.600
them how to perform.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.040
But there was this one girl he brought in
who broke down crying in the middle of one

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:44.400
of our juggling lessons.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.760
She said she wanted to go home.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:50.080
And that's when I knew that Manny had graduated
from saving runaways and abused kids like

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.560
me to abducting kids with powers.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.480
Of course, I confronted him about it - I told
him point-blank, that's not how we do things

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:57.120
anymore.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.280
Herman hadn't been around for years at that
point.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.240
We were better than that.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.760
He gave me some garbage about him doing what
he had to to keep the circus alive.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.680
Can you believe that?

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.960
Even with everything we were doing, he still
thought the circus was on the verge of collapse.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.000
So I took the girl home.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.840
I stored her in my gut and snuck her onto
the Kaleidoscope - That's the machine we use

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.160
to move all the parts of the circus from place
to place.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.960
The kid was back with her parents before dinner,
but when I returned to the circus, Manny was

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:23.760
furious.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:28.480
In hindsight, I think it nearly killed him
to do it, but he said he had to make an example

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:29.120
of me.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:33.040
So he threw me in a box, locked with chains
and painted with the words 'For Essie' on

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:33.600
the top.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.480
I was left out in a field in Kamifurano, Japan,
like a wrapped present for Miss Essie P. And

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.720
that's how I ended up in containment.

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:44.560
Truth be told, I've slept in prisons more
comfortable than this place - the beds are

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.240
hard, the food is gross, and the little red
light on the security camera keeps me up at

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:48.800
night.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.520
Not ideal conditions for a circus superstar&nbsp;

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:54.640
of my caliber, but, as my&nbsp;
old pal Gourdi Lanternskull

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.760
used to say, it's the performer that makes
the stage.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:04.400
Now go check out “Trapped in a Game - SCP-1733
- Season Opener” and “SCP-426 - I am a

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.120
Toaster” for more of the downright weirdest
anomalies out there!

